[{"title":"ArcelorMittal Confirms $1.5 Billion Low-Carbon Steel Investment in France","link":"https://carboncredits.com/arcelormittal-confirms-1-5-billion-low-carbon-steel-investment-in-france/","date":"2026-02-19T13:02:37.643Z","description":"<a href="https://carboncredits.com/arcelormittal-confirms-1-5-billion-low-carbon-steel-investment-in-france/" title="ArcelorMittal Confirms $1.5 Billion Low-Carbon Steel Investment in France" rel="nofollow"><img width="485" height="360" src="https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shutterstock_2423840111-485x360.jpg" class="webfeedsFeaturedVisual wp-post-image" alt="ArcelorMittal Confirms $1.5 Billion Low-Carbon Steel Investment in France" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 5px; clear:both;max-width: 100%;" link_thumbnail="1" decoding="async" /></a><p><span style="font-weight: 400;">ArcelorMittal will invest €1.3 billion (about $1.5 billion) to build a new electric arc furnace (EAF) at its steel site in Dunkirk, France. The company said the project marks a major step in cutting emissions from its French steel production.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The steelmaker announced the decision as French President Emmanuel Macron visited the Dunkirk site. <a href="https://carboncredits.com/steels-heavy-carbon-burden-is-arcelormittal-pulling-back-on-its-net-zero-climate-pledge/">ArcelorMittal</a> said it now has more confidence to move forward because of recent policy and market changes in Europe and France. CEO Geert van Poelvoorde said,</span></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>“The decision to proceed with building an EAF in ArcelorMittal Dunkirk, to produce low-carbon emissions steel at scale for our customers, has been made possible because we now have the conditions in place to make this project a success…We will now focus on steering the Dunkirk EAF project to completion and commercial success.”</em></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The EAF is scheduled to start up in 2029. It will have a capacity of 2 million tonnes of steel per year. </span></p>
<h2><strong>A 2M-Tonne Shift Toward Scrap-Based Steel</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Electric arc furnaces make steel mainly by melting scrap <a href="https://carboncredits.com/building-cleaner-microsoft-and-carbon-direct-launch-eac-guide-for-concrete-and-steel-environmental-attribute-certificates/">steel</a>. They can also use low-carbon inputs like HBI/DRI (hot briquetted iron / direct reduced iron) mixed with hot metal. ArcelorMittal said its Dunkirk EAF will use a mix of scrap, HBI/DRI, and hot metal.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The company also gave a clear emissions estimate. It said the new EAF will emit about 0.6 tonne of CO₂ per tonne of steel and deliver three times less CO₂ than steel made in a blast furnace route.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">This matters because steel is a hard sector to decarbonize. The industry produces significant CO₂e emissions, due to energy-intensive processes and heavy fossil fuel use. </span></p>
